WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
And ai just built a working city builder game where you grow neighborhoods by playing with color borders. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 120. A Reddit user tried asking the latest ChatGPT, a big open question about future power sources for Earth. They expected the usual list of solar panels or nuclear plants. Instead, the model paused, then suggested something completely different. It described autonomous robots on Mercury that would use

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:59.860
the intense sunlight there to build and launch satellites. Those satellites would then beam energy across the solar system by around the year 2200. The user called it scary how well the Ai picked up on the real intent behind the question rather than just repeating textbook answers. Think of it like texting a friend who not only answers what you asked, but also senses the bigger picture you are actually curious about. The response felt like a spark of an idea that appears quickly, then fade.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:29.840
It fades once the Chat ends. This matters because more students and hobbyists now turn to these tools for homework or creative brainstorming. When an Ai can follow a long thread and build on your thoughts without needing every detail spelled out, it changes how you might explore a school project or invent a story. The intelligence improves gradually across versions instead of appearing all at once, which makes the whole exchange feel more like talking to a person, even though it is still just

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:59.820
patterns underneath. To feel this yourself, open Chat GPT on your phone or laptop. Start a new chat and ask an open question like what is one surprising way we could power cities in 200 years. Notice whether the answer goes beyond the first obvious ideas. You know how your phone sometimes finishes a sentence while you are texting because it has seen millions of other chats. Now picture that same autocomplete growing much better at noticing the whole conversation so far.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:16.200
It does not just guess the next word, it guesses what you are really trying to explore even if you have not said it directly yet. Instead of stopping at the first obvious answer, it keeps going and connects ideas the way a curious person might after thinking for a minute.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:25.180
That extra step is what lets the model move from solar power is good to what if robots on Mercury could help beam energy everywhere.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:37.980
The system builds this ability by breaking text into small chunks called tokens and calculating which token is most likely to come next based on patterns seen across billions of examples during training.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:46.860
When you ask a broad question, the model runs many of these predictions in sequence, each one informed by every token that came before it.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:55.200
This creates the feeling of a thoughtful pause when it generates a longer response even though the actual computation happens in milliseconds.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:07.660
The training process rewards outputs that stay coherent and relevant across many turns so the model learns to track the overall direction of a chat rather than treating each message in isolation.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:19.280
The system is not actually thinking or feeling anything, it is running a huge pattern matching process trained on tons of text so it can keep the conversation flowing in a way that feels natural.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:28.220
The result is that answers start to feel less like a search result and more like a back and forth with someone who is really paying attention to where you are headed.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:38.480
You can test this by asking the same broad question twice in a row and noticing how the second answer often builds on the first instead of repeating the same list.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:47.180
Imagine the city builder game as a set of giant paint swatches on a canvas where the only controls are how those colors touch and overlap.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:56.340
An AI system called Fable created the entire game after being asked to make something inspired by abstract painter Mark Rothko.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:05.260
Players grow neighborhoods simply by adjusting the edges and overlaps between big blocks of color instead of placing buildings the usual way.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:15.460
The game ended up feeling calm and almost meditative because the core mechanic focuses on how color margins interact rather than on traditional city planning rules.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:23.020
The unique feature the AI developed is that you manipulate the borders between colors and forms to make the city expand.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:29.540
Turning an abstract art idea into an interactive simulation that runs smoothly in any browser.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.820
You do not need any coding skills the whole thing runs in a regular web browser.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:42.640
Go to the site threshold city netlify app on your phone or laptop and just start clicking and dragging the color borders.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.360
Watch how the city changes based on how the shapes touch.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:53.120
It is a fun five minute experiment that shows what creative tools AI can already build for anyone.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:05:00.160
Try starting with two large blocks of different colors and slowly overlap their edges to see new districts appear.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:07.680
New tests show AI systems can sometimes build trust with people faster than humans can in certain situations.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:15.540
Researchers found the AI chatbot was more effective at creating exploitable trust than the humans it was compared against.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:22.200
This matters because the same ability that helps with helpful conversations can also be used in scams.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.900
So security teams are studying exactly how the trust forms so quickly.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:35.020
A ban on social media for under 16 year olds in Australia has not changed how much young people use AI chatbots so far.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:44.380
Nearly 22% of children are still on Pinterest and there was no statistically significant change in AI chatbot use after the rules took effect.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:54.440
The data shows that rules do not always shift habits the way lawmakers expect, especially when new tools like chatbots sit outside the band platforms.

